@node CMacs Context
@chapter CMacs Context: Named Arrangements You Switch Between

@cindex contexts
@cindex named arrangements
@cindex cmacs-context
@cindex switching contexts

@code{cmacs-journal-restore} answers ``put me back where I was at
@var{time}'' (@pxref{CMacs Journal Restore}).  @code{cmacs-context}
answers ``switch me to @var{work}''.  A context is a named arrangement:
the Emacs window tree, the open files and pages, and under gowl the
applications, their tags, and each monitor's view and layout.  You save
one, switch to it, update it and list them.

@example
M-x cmacs-context-save RET work RET        capture what is up now
M-x cmacs-context-switch RET research RET  go somewhere else
M-x cmacs-context-list                     all of them, with keys
@end example

It is not a second implementation of anything.  An arrangement is a
checkpoint snapshot (@code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-snapshot}, the
format the journal's checkpoints use).  A switch is a restore plan built
from that snapshot (@code{cmacs-journal-restore-plan-from-snapshot}) and
carried out by the restore's executor
(@code{cmacs-journal-restore-execute-plan}).  The plan buffer you are
shown is the restore's plan buffer.

Five things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{The journal is not involved.}  A context is saved and switched
to with @code{cmacs-journal-mode} off, and saving writes nothing to the
journal.  The only thing a context ever writes there is one event per
switch, and only while the journal is already recording
(@pxref{Context State}).

@item
@strong{A switch adds and arranges.  It never closes.}  No buffer is
killed and no application's window is closed, ever.  Windows that belong
to neither context stay where they are.

@item
@strong{An application is started again, not resumed.}  As with a
restore: its documents, tabs and unsaved work are its own business.

@item
@strong{The journal's filters decide what a saved context holds},
because the snapshot is the checkpoint's.  A buffer, page or window your
rules exclude is not in the file, and when the rule set cannot be loaded
nothing is saved at all.

@item
@strong{It works without the compositor.}  In a pgtk or tty Emacs a
context is its Emacs half: buffers, pages and the window tree.  Every
gowl call is behind a check that gowl is running.
@end itemize

Pure Elisp, in every build.  Files: @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-context.el},
@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-context-tools.el}; tests in
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-context-tests.el}.

