@node Context Kinds
@section Two Kinds of Context

@subsection Saved

@findex cmacs-context-save
@vindex cmacs-context-directory
@vindex cmacs-context-save-include
@code{cmacs-context-save @var{name}} captures the live arrangement into
@code{cmacs-context-directory} (@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/contexts},
mode 0700), one @file{@var{name}.json} per context (mode 0600, written
to a temporary file and renamed).  The file is JSON because that is what
the checkpoint encoder produces and Emacs has no YAML writer; it is
pretty-printed, and its @code{snapshot} member is exactly a checkpoint
document.

With a prefix argument the save asks what to include.  The categories
are those of @code{cmacs-context-save-include}:

@table @code
@item buffers
the Emacs window tree and the open file buffers
@item urls
the pages in gsurf buffers
@item apps
gowl's windows: app id, title, tags, monitor, floating, command line
@item layout
each monitor's tags in view and layout
@end table

A save captures every application window on every tag, not only the
tags in view: a context is an arrangement of the desktop, and one that
spans tags 1 to 3 must not lose tag 3 because you were looking at tag 1
when you left.

The first save of a context also records a directory for it---the
project root of what is selected, else its directory---unless the
context declares one.  Later saves keep it.

@subsection Declared

@findex cmacs-context-define
@lisp
(cmacs-context-define "work"
  :directory "~/source/projects/cmacs"
  :project "cmacs"
  :files '("AGENTS.md" "src/emacs.c")
  :urls '("https://example.org/dashboard")
  :apps '("foot"                        ; a command; app id "foot"
          (:command "librewolf --new-window" :app "librewolf" :tag 2)
          (:command "foot -T scratch" :app "foot" :tag 3 :floating t
           :geometry (100 100 900 600)))
  :tags '(1)
  :layout "tile"
  :env '(("CMACS_DEBUG" . "1"))
  :setup (lambda () (message "at work"))
  :teardown #'my/stop-work-timers)
@end lisp

@table @code
@item :files
Files to open; a relative name is under @code{:directory}.  The first is
shown on arriving.
@item :urls
Pages to open.
@item :apps
A command string, or a plist of @code{:command}, @code{:app},
@code{:tag}, @code{:tags}, @code{:floating}, @code{:monitor} and
@code{:geometry}.  @code{:app} is the window's app id, by which a
running one is recognised; it defaults to the command's name.  With no
tag an application goes to the context's @code{:tags}, else tag 1.
@item :tags
The tags to view on switching.
@item :layout
The gowl layout to set, by name.  With no @code{:tags} it applies to the
tags in view.
@item :directory
Where the context lives.
@item :project
A label for the list and the journal.
@item :label
The name the journal records, if not the context's.
@item :env
Variables for the applications this context @emph{starts}.
@item :setup
A function of no argument, called after arriving.
@item :teardown
A function of no argument, called before leaving.
@item :auto-save
Overrides @code{cmacs-context-auto-save-on-leave} for this context.
@end table

An unknown key is an error, not something ignored.

@vindex cmacs-context-files
@findex cmacs-context-reload-files
The same data can live in YAML, read through
@code{cmacs-yaml-parse-file} when the build has it.  List the files in
@code{cmacs-context-files}; they are read the first time contexts are
asked for, and again by @kbd{M-x cmacs-context-reload-files}.

@example
contexts:
  research:
    directory: ~/Documents/notes
    tags: [4]
    urls:
      - https://example.org/paper
    apps:
      - command: foot
        tag: 4
    env:
      TOPIC: lasers
@end example

Functions cannot be named from YAML: @code{setup} and @code{teardown}
there are errors, and so is any other key this does not know.  A file
that fails is reported in a warning and the rest are still read.

@subsection Precedence, When a Context Is Both

A declared context becomes ``both'' the moment it is saved---by
@code{cmacs-context-update}, or by leaving it with auto-save on.

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Arrangement: the saved state wins, category by category.}  A
category the saved state holds @emph{replaces} the declaration's; it is
not merged with it.  Close an application, leave, come back: it is not
started again.  A category the save did not include still comes from the
declaration.

@item
@strong{Everything else comes from the declaration} whenever it is
declared there: @code{:directory}, @code{:project}, @code{:label},
@code{:env}, @code{:setup}, @code{:teardown}.  The saved directory is
used only when the declaration names none.

@item
@strong{@code{cmacs-context-delete} removes the saved state and leaves
the declaration}, which is how you get back to what your init says.  The
list shows which contexts are @code{declared}, @code{saved} or
@code{both}.
@end itemize

The consequence to know about: once a declared context has been saved,
editing its @code{:files} or @code{:apps} in your init changes nothing
you can see until you delete the saved state.  If you want a declaration
to stay authoritative, give it @code{:auto-save nil} and do not update
it.

@node Context Commands
@section Commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-context-save @var{name}
Capture now as @var{name} and be in it; @kbd{C-u} asks what to include.
@item cmacs-context-switch @var{name}
Switch; @kbd{C-u} shows the plan first.
@item cmacs-context-update
Save the arrangement into the context you are in.
@item cmacs-context-diff @var{name}
The plan a switch to @var{name} would carry out; changes nothing.
@item cmacs-context-delete @var{name}
Delete the saved state (a declaration remains).
@item cmacs-context-rename @var{name} @var{new}
Rename a saved context (a declared one is renamed in your init).
@item cmacs-context-list
A table: @kbd{RET} or @kbd{s} switch, @kbd{S} save over, @kbd{d} diff,
@kbd{D} delete, @kbd{r} rename, @kbd{+} new, @kbd{g} refresh.
@item cmacs-context-menu
The switcher: gowl's menu card, else the minibuffer.
@item cmacs-context-cancel
Stop the switch in flight.
@item cmacs-context-log
What the last switch did, step by step.
@item cmacs-context-project-find-file
@code{project-find-file} in the context's directory.
@item cmacs-context-compile
@code{compile} in the context's directory.
@end table

The switcher lists the most recently used context first and the current
one last, so the first candidate is where you were before.  Each
candidate is annotated: applications, files, project, when it was last
used.

A context's name is one line of at most 64 characters with no slash, not
starting with a dot.  It becomes a file name (percent-encoded).

@node Context Switching
@section What a Switch Does

In order:

@enumerate
@item
A switch that is in flight is cancelled.

@item
@strong{Leaving.}  If you were in a context and had fully arrived at it:
its @code{:teardown} runs, and with
@code{cmacs-context-auto-save-on-leave} (default on) the arrangement you
are leaving is saved into it.  That is what makes contexts drift with
you the way workspaces do.  A context whose own switch was cancelled
part-way is neither torn down nor saved: it would be saved half-arrived.

@item
@strong{Arriving.}  The context becomes current; its directory becomes
the default @code{default-directory}
(@code{cmacs-context-set-default-directory}); the journal is told, if it
is recording; the mode line and the bar show the name; integrations are
entered; other windows are parked, if you asked for that.

@item
@strong{The plan is carried out} by the restore's executor, one step per
timer: files, pages, the Emacs window tree, applications (running ones
moved to their tags, missing ones started and pre-tagged so they never
flash on the tag in view), each monitor's view and layout, focus.

@item
@code{:setup} runs, then @code{cmacs-context-after-switch-functions}
with the new and the previous name.
@end enumerate

Switching to the context you are already in puts its saved arrangement
back @emph{without} saving the current one first: it is how you undo a
mess.

@subsection Fast, and When It Asks

@vindex cmacs-context-confirm-first-switch
@vindex cmacs-context-confirm-launches
A switch has to be as fast as changing tags or nobody uses it, so it is
immediate.  The plan is shown for confirming only:

@itemize @bullet
@item
before the @emph{first} switch to a context
(@code{cmacs-context-confirm-first-switch});
@item
when it would start more than @code{cmacs-context-confirm-launches}
applications (default 3);
@item
with a prefix argument.
@end itemize

Confirming in the plan buffer (@kbd{c}) switches; every line there can
be turned off first.

@subsection Asynchronous and Cancellable

@code{cmacs-context-switch} returns before a file is visited.  Each step
runs from a zero-delay timer, and the windows of started applications
are placed when they appear, from the restore's poll timer.  Nothing
waits: under @command{emacs --gowl} Emacs is the compositor and a wait
for a window would be waiting for itself.

A second switch while one is in flight stops the first between steps.
What the first had done stays done---files visited stay open, programs
started stay started, on the tags they were started for---but nothing
more is opened, moved or focused, its @code{:setup} does not run, and
its pending windows are no longer looked for.  @kbd{M-x
cmacs-context-cancel} does the same without switching.

@subsection Windows That Belong to Neither Context

@vindex cmacs-context-park-others
Left alone, on their tags.  With @code{cmacs-context-park-others} set to
a tag number, a running window that is not part of the context being
switched to @emph{and} sits on one of that context's tags is moved to
the parking tag, so the context's tags show only the context.  It is not
moved back, and it is never closed.

@subsection Emacs Buffers and Perspectives

@vindex cmacs-context-use-perspectives
@findex cmacs-context-register-integration
No buffer is killed by a switch.  If @code{persp-mode} is on (Doom's
workspaces), a context maps to the perspective of the same name: it is
switched to---and made, if new---before the context's buffers are
opened, so they land in it.  This is a registered, optional integration
(@code{cmacs-context-use-perspectives}); nothing requires persp-mode.
Your own goes in with:

@lisp
(cmacs-context-register-integration 'my-tabs
  :when  (lambda () tab-bar-mode)
  :enter (lambda (name) (tab-bar-switch-to-tab name)))
@end lisp

@code{:when}, @code{:enter} and @code{:leave} all run under
@code{condition-case}.

@node Context State
@section What a Context Carries Besides a Layout

@subsection A Directory

@findex cmacs-context-directory-now
@code{:directory} (or the directory recorded at the first save) is:

@itemize @bullet
@item
the default value of @code{default-directory} while you are in the
context, so buffers that visit no file start there (the original is put
back in a context with no directory);
@item
where the applications the context @emph{starts} are started---a
terminal opens in the project;
@item
what @code{cmacs-context-project-find-file} and
@code{cmacs-context-compile} use, whatever buffer is current, and what
@code{(cmacs-context-directory-now)} returns for your own commands.
@end itemize

A plain @code{project-find-file} or @code{compile} still goes by the
current buffer's directory, as it always has; the two commands above are
the ones that go by the context.

@subsection Environment Variables

@code{:env} is set for the applications a context starts, by starting
them through @command{env} (@code{gowl-spawn} runs a command line, not a
shell).  The values:

@itemize @bullet
@item
come from your Elisp or YAML and from nowhere else---nothing is captured
from running programs;
@item
are @emph{never} written to a saved state file;
@item
are visible in the plan buffer and in the process list, like any command
line.
@end itemize

They are not a place for secrets.

@subsection Hooks

@code{:setup} and @code{:teardown} run on the main thread under
@code{condition-case}.  One that signals is reported in the echo area
and the switch goes on.

@subsection The Journal

If---and only if---the journal is recording, a switch writes @emph{one}
ordinary event:

@table @code
@item kind
@code{context}
@item title
the context's @code{:label}, else its name
@item project
the context's project
@item detail
@code{switch}
@end table

It goes through the journal's normal filtered write path, so a rule can
rewrite the name or drop the event (@code{kind: context}) like any
other; no kind needs registering.  A report can group time by context
from these events: a context lasts from its event to the next one.
Saving a context writes nothing.

@node Context Doors
@section Doors

@subsection A gowl Key

@findex cmacs-context-setup
@vindex cmacs-context-key
@code{cmacs-context-setup} binds @code{cmacs-context-key} (default
@kbd{Super+Ctrl+c}) to the switcher.  cmacs calls it when the desktop
starts.  The switcher uses the compositor's menu card
(@code{menu-choose}), so it comes up over whatever application has the
keyboard; without the menu module it falls back to the minibuffer.

@subsection The Mode Line and the Bar

@vindex cmacs-context-mode-line-format
@vindex cmacs-context-bar-widget
@vindex cmacs-context-bar-slot
The current context is shown in the mode line
(@code{cmacs-context-mode-line-format}, through
@code{global-mode-string}).  Under gowl it is also pushed to the bar: to
the slot @code{cmacs-context-bar-slot} when the bar has named slots
(@code{cmacs-gowl-bar-slot-set}), otherwise as the text of the widget
@code{cmacs-context-bar-widget} (default the shipped @code{label}).
That widget is one item; if an LQL watch already drives it, set the
option to @code{nil}.

@subsection Voice

@table @asis
@item ``switch to work'', ``go back to research''
Switch (only for names that are contexts).
@item ``switch to context work'', ``switch to the work context''
Switch.
@item ``save this as research''
Save as that name, new or existing.
@item ``update this context''
Save into the current one.
@end table

The bare ``switch to @var{name}'' is only claimed for names that are
contexts---``switch to firefox'' is still a window---so that command's
pattern is rebuilt whenever the set of contexts changes.  A switch that
needs confirming shows its plan and asks the voice way.

@subsection Tools

@file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-context-tools.el}, on every capability surface:

@table @code
@item context_list
The contexts, one per line.
@item context_current
The one you are in, or @code{none}.
@item context_switch
Switch; @code{:destructive}, and the person is asked first.
@end table

A context's name is your own text, so every name shown to a caller goes
through @code{cmacs-redact-text} (@pxref{CMacs Redact}), and
@code{context_switch} accepts a name in the form it was shown: a model
can switch to a context without being told what it is really called.  A
name your rules withhold is not listed and cannot be switched to through
a tool---with the answer a name that does not exist gets.

An agent or a remote caller cannot call @code{cmacs-context-switch} or
@code{cmacs-context-save} directly: a switch starts programs, and the
tool's confirmation is what stands in for the person.  A switch reached
through the tool never shows the plan buffer, because nothing on that
path may prompt.

@node Context Options
@section Options

@table @code
@item cmacs-context-directory
Where saved contexts are kept.  Default
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/contexts}.
@item cmacs-context-files
YAML files declaring contexts.  Default @code{nil}.
@item cmacs-context-auto-save-on-leave
Save the context you leave into itself.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-context-park-others
A tag for other windows on the context's tags.  Default @code{nil}.
@item cmacs-context-confirm-first-switch
Show the plan before a first switch.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-context-confirm-launches
Show the plan above this many applications to start.  Default 3.
@item cmacs-context-save-include
What a save captures.  Default @code{(buffers urls apps layout)}.
@item cmacs-context-set-default-directory
Make the context's directory the default one.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-context-use-perspectives
Map contexts to persp-mode perspectives.  Default @code{t}.
@item cmacs-context-key
The gowl key for the switcher.  Default @kbd{Super+Ctrl+c}.
@item cmacs-context-mode-line-format
The mode line text, or @code{nil}.  Default @code{" [%s]"}.
@item cmacs-context-bar-widget
The bar widget to push to, until slots exist.  Default @code{"label"}.
@item cmacs-context-bar-slot
The bar slot, when slots exist.  Default @code{"context"}.
@item cmacs-context-after-switch-functions
Called with the new and the previous name.  Default @code{nil}.
@end table

The restore's own options apply to a switch too:
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-app-timeout},
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-app-commands} (how to start an application,
overriding the recorded command) and
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-layout-names}.

@node Context Journal Additions
@section What Was Added to the Journal's Files

Three public functions and one alias in
@file{cmacs-journal-restore.el}, all additive;
@file{cmacs-journal-checkpoint.el} is unchanged.

@table @code
@item cmacs-journal-restore-plan-from-snapshot @var{snapshot} &rest @var{props}
A plan from a snapshot that need not come from the journal.
@code{:title}, @code{:notes}, @code{:execute} and @code{:refresh} let
the plan buffer serve another caller.
@item cmacs-journal-restore-execute-plan @var{plan} &rest @var{options}
The executor without the ``is this an agent'' refusal, for a caller that
has done its own asking.  @code{:async}, @code{:quiet}, @code{:on-done}.
@code{cmacs-journal-restore-execute} is now that refusal followed by
this.
@item cmacs-journal-restore-cancel @var{plan}
Stop a plan between steps and stop looking for its windows.
@item cmacs-journal-restore-show-plan @var{plan}
The plan buffer.
@end table
